

================================================================================
                    FAST AND SLOW POINTER TECHNIQUE
================================================================================

1. What is Fast and Slow Pointer Technique?

    - Fast and Slow Pointer is a technique used to traverse a Linked List
      using two pointers simultaneously.

    - Both pointers initially point to the head node of the Linked List.

    - Slow Pointer moves one node at a time.

    - Fast Pointer moves two nodes at a time.

    - Since Fast Pointer moves twice as fast as Slow Pointer,
      when Fast Pointer reaches the end of the Linked List,
      Slow Pointer reaches the middle node.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

2. How Does It Work?

    - Initially, both Fast and Slow pointers point to the head node.

    - In every iteration:
        1. Fast Pointer moves 2 nodes.
        2. Slow Pointer moves 1 node.

    - The loop continues until Fast Pointer or Fast Pointer's next
      becomes null.

    - When the loop stops, Slow Pointer points to the required node.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

3. Diagram (Even Number of Nodes)

    Linked List:

    1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 4 -> 5 -> 6 -> null

    Initially:

    Slow
     |
     v
    [1] -> [2] -> [3] -> [4] -> [5] -> [6] -> null
     ^
     |
    Fast


    After 1st Iteration:

           Slow
            |
            v
    [1] -> [2] -> [3] -> [4] -> [5] -> [6] -> null
     ^
     |
    Fast has moved to node 3.


    After 2nd Iteration:

                   Slow
                    |
                    v
    [1] -> [2] -> [3] -> [4] -> [5] -> [6] -> null
                              ^
                              |
                         Fast is at node 5.


    After 3rd Iteration:

                          Slow
                           |
                           v
    [1] -> [2] -> [3] -> [4] -> [5] -> [6] -> null
                                               
    Fast moves from node 5 to node 6 and then becomes null.

    Final Position:

        Slow Pointer = Node 4
        Fast Pointer = null

    Therefore, Node 4 is returned as the middle node.

    NOTE:
    If the Linked List contains an even number of nodes,
    Slow Pointer returns the SECOND middle node.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

4. Why Do We Use This Condition?

    while (fastPointer != null && fastPointer.next != null)

    - fastPointer != null:

      Check karta hai ki Fast Pointer kisi valid node par hai.

    - fastPointer.next != null:

      Check karta hai ki Fast Pointer ke aage ek aur node available hai.

    - Dono conditions isliye check karte hain kyunki Fast Pointer ko
      ek iteration mein 2 steps move karna hota hai.

    - Agar fastPointer ya fastPointer.next null hua,
      toh loop stop ho jayega.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

5. Code Implementation:

    ListNode fastPointer = head;
    ListNode slowPointer = head;

    while (fastPointer != null && fastPointer.next != null) {

        fastPointer = fastPointer.next.next; // Moves 2 nodes
        slowPointer = slowPointer.next;      // Moves 1 node
    }

    return slowPointer;

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

6. Applications of Fast and Slow Pointer Technique:

    1. Finding the Middle Node of a Linked List.
    2. Detecting a Cycle in a Linked List.
    3. Finding the Starting Point of a Cycle.
    4. Finding the Nth Node from the End (using a gap between pointers).
    5. Checking whether a Linked List is a Palindrome.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

7. Time and Space Complexity:

    Time Complexity  : O(n)
    Space Complexity : O(1)

    - O(n): Linked List ko traverse karne ke liye.
    - O(1): Only two pointers are used, no extra data structure.

